  "text": "To find the minimum value of \\( c \\) such that there exists a unique real solution to the system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx^2 + y^2 = 1 \\\\\ny = |x - a| + |x - b| + |x - c|\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( a < b < c \\) are positive integers, we need to analyze the behavior of the function \\( y = |x - a| + |x - b| + |x - c| \\).\n\nThe function \\( y = |x - a| + |x - b| + |x - c| \\) can be broken down into linear segments based on the values of \\( x \\):\n\n1. For \\( -\\infty < x < a \\), \\( y = (a - x) + (b - x) + (c - x) = (a + b + c) - 3x \\).\n2. For \\( a \\leq x < b \\), \\( y = (x - a) + (b - x) + (c - x) = (b + c - a) - x \\).\n3. For \\( b \\leq x < c \\), \\( y = (x - a) + (x - b) + (c - x) = (c - a) + (c - b) = 2c - a - b \\).\n4. For \\( x \\geq c \\), \\( y = (x - a) + (x - b) + (x - c) = 3x - (a + b + c) \\).\n\nWe need to find the minimum value of \\( c \\) such that there exists a unique real solution. Let's analyze the behavior of the function in the range \\( b \\leq x < c \\):\n\nThe function \\( y = 2c - a - b \\) is constant in this range. For the system of equations to have a unique solution, the line \\( y = 2c - a - b \\) must intersect the circle \\( x^2 + y^2 = 1 \\) at exactly one point in the range \\( b \\leq x < c \\). This means that the value \\( 2c - a - b \\) must be either equal to 1 (the radius of the circle) or it must intersect the circle tangentially, giving us one point of intersection.\n\nThe equation \\( 2c - a - b = 1 \\) gives us the condition for a unique solution:\n\n\\[\n2c = a + b + 1\n\\]\n\nThe minimum value of \\( c \\) is obtained when \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are minimized. The smallest possible values for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are 1 and 2, respectively. Plugging these values into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n2c = 1 + 2 + 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\nc = 2\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this:\n\nIf \\( c = 2 \\), then \\( 2c = 4 \\) and \\( a + b + 1 = 4 \\). This gives us the condition \\( 2c = a + b + 1 \\) as derived earlier. For \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\), we have \\( 4 = 1 + 2 + 1 \\), which is true. The function \\( y = 2c - a - b = 2 \\cdot 2 - 1 - 2 = 1 \\), which is the radius of the circle, giving us one point of intersection.\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
